## Heaters: the name, how it heats, the description.
heater-electric-hotplate-phrase = on an electric hob
heater-electric-hotplate = Electric hob (cast-iron plate)
heater-electric-hotplate-description-novice = Inside the cast-iron disc there is a heating coil. Electric current heats the disc, and the disc passes heat to the bottom of the pot by touch. The disc itself warms up and cools down slowly.
heater-electric-hotplate-description-school = Current heats a coil inside a cast-iron disc (Joule heating). The disc passes heat to the vessel by conduction through the contact area; part of the heat leaves the hot disc into the air.
heater-induction-hob-phrase = on an induction hob
heater-induction-hob = Induction hob
heater-induction-hob-description-novice = A coil is hidden under the glass. Its changing magnetic field creates electric currents right in the bottom of the pot — so the bottom heats itself. That is why the hob only heats pots made of a magnetic metal.
heater-induction-hob-description-school = Alternating current in a coil makes a changing magnetic field; it induces eddy currents in the base of a ferromagnetic pot (cast iron, magnetic steels), which heats it. Aluminium and copper are usually not heated.
heater-gas-hob-phrase = on a kitchen gas hob
heater-gas-hob = Gas hob (kitchen burner)
heater-gas-hob-description-novice = Natural gas burns. Hot flame gases flow around the bottom of the pot, but much of the heat goes past it into the room.
heater-gas-hob-description-school = Burning gas releases heat. The pot receives only part of it — about 30–44 % by measurements; the rest heats the air around.
heater-immersion-heater-phrase = with an immersion heater
heater-immersion-heater = Immersion heater
heater-immersion-heater-description-novice = The heating element goes straight into the water, so almost all the heat goes into the water.
heater-immersion-heater-description-school = Current heats a metal tube with an insulated coil inside; the tube gives heat to the water surrounding it on all sides. Little is lost to the vessel and the air.
heater-immersion-heater-safety-school = Never switch an immersion heater on outside water: the water must cover the heating part.
heater-bunsen-burner-phrase = over a Bunsen burner
heater-bunsen-burner = Bunsen burner
heater-bunsen-burner-description-novice = A laboratory gas burner. A valve sets how much gas flows, a collar sets how much air mixes in — this changes the flame.
heater-bunsen-burner-description-school = Gas mixes with air inside the tube and burns above it. The valve sets the gas flow and therefore the power; vessels stand on a tripod above the flame.
heater-spirit-lamp-phrase = over a spirit lamp
heater-spirit-lamp = Spirit lamp
heater-spirit-lamp-description-novice = Alcohol rises up a wick and burns with a small flame. Spirit lamps heat test tubes and small amounts of liquid.
heater-spirit-lamp-description-school = A spirit lamp has low power, and we found no reliable measurement — it is an estimate here. You can measure it yourself: heat a known mass of water and compute Q = c·m·Δt.
